      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To find the probability that when we roll four fair 6-sided dice, they won't all show the same number, we can use the complementary probability method. This means we first calculate the probability that all four dice show the same number, and then subtract this from 1.\n\n1. **Calculate the total number of possible outcomes when rolling four dice:**\n   Each die has 6 faces, so the total number of possible outcomes is:\n   \\[\n   6^4 = 1296\n   \\]\n\n2. **Calculate the number of outcomes where all four dice show the same number:**\n   There are 6 possible outcomes where all four dice show the same number (one for each number from 1 to 6):\n   \\[\n   6\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the probability that all four dice show the same number:**\n   This probability is the number of favorable outcomes divided by the total number of possible outcomes:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{6}{1296} = \\frac{1}{216}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Calculate the probability that not all four dice show the same number:**\n   This is the complement of the probability that all four dice show the same number:\n   \\[\n   1 - \\frac{1}{216} = \\frac{215}{216}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the probability that when we roll four fair 6-sided dice, they won't all show the same number is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{215}{216}}\n\\]",
